use std
into em.amni
dialect js

This file is a library built for the web as a wasm module; its `main()` does nothing. JavaScript drives it through its exported functions (every name starting with em_ is exported). After JavaScript solves a scalar potential (electric potential in volts) on the Amni-CAD voxel grid with its multigrid solver, this module turns the nodal potential into the field per element (the potential gradient, in volts per millimeter), the power or energy each element holds, a nodal average of the field for coloring, the heat each node receives for a coupled thermal solve, and field statistics. Every length is in millimeters.

```amni
extern fn em_view_f64(id i32, xs real[:]) = "em_view_f64";
extern fn em_view_i32(id i32, xs i32[:]) = "em_view_i32";
```

Grid layout. The grid has `EX`, `EY`, `EZ` elements along x, y and z; element e is (k times `EY` plus j) times `EX` plus i. Nodes form a grid of `NX` equal to `EX` plus 1, `NY` equal to `EY` plus 1 and `NZ` equal to `EZ` plus 1, with node (i, j, k) numbered (k times `NY` plus j) times `NX` plus i. The 8 nodes of element (i, j, k), with n0 its node (i, j, k), are in this order: n0, n0 plus 1, n0 plus `NX` plus 1, n0 plus `NX`, then the same four plus `NX` times `NY`. Nodes 1, 2, 5 and 6 are on the plus x side, nodes 2, 3, 6 and 7 on the plus y side, nodes 4, 5, 6 and 7 on the plus z side.

Module state. Ints, all starting at 0: `EX`, `EY`, `EZ`, `NX`, `NY`, `NZ`, `NE` (element count), `NN` (node count). Growable f64 arrays: `PH` (potential per node), `EF` (field magnitude per element), `GV` (3 per element, the field vector, minus the gradient), `NF` (field magnitude per node, averaged), `NQ` (power per node), `NW` (element count per node, as a real), `HG` (histogram bins), `FR` (fill fraction per element, between 0 and 1), `EN` (3 per node, the summed outward normal of the electrode faces at that node, zero at other nodes), `DN` (solver density per element), `FN` (3 entries, the fill normal of the last element asked), `PR` (8 parameters), `ST` (8 statistics). Growable i32 arrays: `MK` (per element, 1 inside the part, 0 outside), `EI` (per node, the electrode index, minus 1 where the node is free), `EJ` (per node, scratch copy of `EI`).

Parameters in `PR`: 0 element size h in millimeters, 1 material coefficient c (electrical conductivity in siemens per millimeter, or permittivity in farads per millimeter), 2 histogram bin count (at least 16). Statistics in `ST`: 0 largest field magnitude, 1 the 99.5th percentile field magnitude, 2 the sum over the part of c times the solver density times the squared gradient magnitude times h cubed (watts for conduction, twice the stored energy in joules for electrostatics), 3 element count in the part, 4 mean field magnitude over the part weighted by fill fraction, 5 the sum of the fill fractions of the part elements.

Sizing helpers. `zf(xs, n, x)` takes an f64 growable array by reference: xs is emptied, then gets `reserve`(n), then is resized to n entries equal to x. `zi(xs, n, x)` does the same for an i32 growable array, resizing to n entries equal to x as i32. Every array in this file is sized only through these two helpers.

The exported function `em_views()`, declared with the export keyword, registers every array JavaScript reads or writes: f64 views 1 `PH`, 2 `EF`, 3 `NF`, 4 `NQ`, 5 `PR`, 6 `ST`, 7 `GV`, 9 `FR`, 10 `EN`, 11 `DN`; i32 views 8 `MK` and 12 `EI`; through em_view_f64 and em_view_i32.

The exported function `em_grid(ex, ey, ez)`, declared with the export keyword, takes three i32 element counts, sets `EX`, `EY`, `EZ`, `NX`, `NY`, `NZ`, `NE` (ex times ey times ez) and `NN` (`NX` times `NY` times `NZ`), sizes with the helpers `EF` to `NE` zeros, `GV` to 3 times `NE` zeros, `MK` to `NE` zeros, `FR` to `NE` zeros, `DN` to `NE` zeros, `PH`, `NF`, `NQ` and `NW` to `NN` zeros, `EN` to 3 times `NN` zeros, `EI` and `EJ` to `NN` entries of minus 1, `FN` to 3 zeros, `HG` to 16 zeros, `PR` and `ST` to 8 zeros, then calls `em_views` and returns `NE`.

`fillof(i, j, k)` takes three i32 element indices and returns FR of element (k times `EY` plus j) times `EX` plus i as a real when the indices are inside the grid (0 up to `EX`, `EY` and `EZ` minus 1) and `MK` of that element is 1, and 0.0 otherwise.

`fnorm(e)` takes an i32 element index, with i equal to e mod `EX`, j equal to (e div `EX`) mod `EY` and k equal to e div (`EX` times `EY`); dx is (fillof(i plus 1, j, k) minus fillof(i minus 1, j, k)) divided by 2.0, dy is (fillof(i, j plus 1, k) minus fillof(i, j minus 1, k)) divided by 2.0, dz is (fillof(i, j, k plus 1) minus fillof(i, j, k minus 1)) divided by 2.0; when the length of (dx, dy, dz) is above 1e-9, `FN` gets minus dx, minus dy and minus dz each divided by that length (the outward normal) and it returns 1; otherwise `FN` gets three zeros and it returns 0.

The exported function `em_prep()`, declared with the export keyword, runs after JavaScript filled `MK`, `FR`, `EI` and `EN`, and does two things. First it moves each electrode onto the part surface: `EJ` gets a copy of `EI`; for each node n with `EJ`[n] at least 0 and an `EN` vector of length above 1e-9, a is the axis (0 for x, 1 for y, 2 for z) with the largest absolute `EN` component at n (the first one on ties) and s is 1 when that component is positive and minus 1 otherwise; with (i, j, k) the node position (n mod `NX`, (n div `NX`) mod `NY`, n div (`NX` times `NY`)), m is the node one step along axis a in direction s; when m is inside the node grid, `EJ`[m] is minus 1, and at least one of the 4 elements that hold both n and m is in the part (`MK` 1; for a step along x from node (i, j, k) to (i plus s, j, k) these are the elements with x index the smaller of i and i plus s, y index j minus 1 or j and z index k minus 1 or k, skipping indices outside the grid, and likewise for y and z), then `EI`[m] gets `EJ`[n], `EI`[n] gets minus 1, and the 3 `EN` entries of n move to m (n gets zeros). Second it sets the solver density `DN` of every element e: 0.0 when `MK`[e] is 0; otherwise f is FR[e], and DN[e] is the larger of f and 0.12; then when f is below 1.0, fnorm(e) returns 1, and one of the 8 nodes of e (in the node order above) has `EI` at least 0 and an `EN` vector whose dot product with `FN`, divided by the length of that `EN` vector, has absolute value above 0.7, DN[e] becomes 1.0 divided by the larger of f and 0.12 (a partly filled element between an electrode and the inside carries the flux across its short thickness). It returns the number of nodes with `EI` at least 0.

The exported function `em_fields()`, declared with the export keyword, runs after JavaScript filled `PR` and `PH` and after `em_prep`; every int used in real math is converted with `as real`, and a real used as an int with `as int`, since Helium has no real or int conversion calls. h is PR[0] and c is PR[1]. It zeroes `NF`, `NQ`, `NW` and `ST`. For each element e with `MK`[e] equal to 1, with i equal to e mod `EX`, j equal to (e div `EX`) mod `EY`, k equal to e div (`EX` times `EY`) and n0 its node (k times `NY` plus j) times `NX` plus i, it reads the 8 nodal potentials p0 to p7 in the node order above; the gradient is gx equal to ((p1 plus p2 plus p5 plus p6) minus (p0 plus p3 plus p4 plus p7)) divided by 4h, gy equal to ((p2 plus p3 plus p6 plus p7) minus (p0 plus p1 plus p4 plus p5)) divided by 4h, gz equal to ((p4 plus p5 plus p6 plus p7) minus (p0 plus p1 plus p2 plus p3)) divided by 4h; the element power pe is c times DN[e] times (gx squared plus gy squared plus gz squared) times h cubed (the energy the solver sees). Then the interface correction: d is DN[e]; when d is not 1.0 and fnorm(e) returns 1, gn is gx times FN[0] plus gy times FN[1] plus gz times FN[2], and gx, gy and gz each gain (d minus 1.0) times gn times FN[0], FN[1] and FN[2] respectively, so the part of the gradient across the boundary is scaled by d (the normal flux stays continuous) while the part along it is kept. GV at 3e, 3e plus 1 and 3e plus 2 gets minus gx, minus gy and minus gz; EF[e] is the square root of gx squared plus gy squared plus gz squared; ST[2] grows by pe, ST[3] by 1, ST[4] by FR[e] times EF[e], ST[5] by FR[e], ST[0] becomes the larger of itself and EF[e]; each of its 8 nodes gets NF plus EF[e], NQ plus pe divided by 8 and NW plus 1. Elements not in the part get EF 0.0 and a zero vector. Then each node with NW above 0.0 gets NF divided by NW. ST[4] is divided by the larger of 1e-12 and ST[5]. Then the percentile: nb is PR[2] as int, at least 16; `HG` is sized with `zf` to nb zeros; when ST[0] is above 0.0, each part element adds 1 to the bin the smaller of nb minus 1 and (EF[e] divided by ST[0] times nb) as int; walking the bins from the top, it accumulates counts until they exceed 0.005 times ST[3], and ST[1] is the lower edge of that bin (its index divided by nb times ST[0]); with no field ST[1] is 0.0. It returns ST[2].


`main()` does nothing.
